 us, we are taking you by the hand and we shall not drop you!\par \par For the moment, }{\b\f210 the important thing is to mark the accentuation well}{\f208  [this is of course a remark for native French speakers, who don't have a movable accent in their language], that is to say, to pronounce the letters in }{\b\f210 bold}{\f208  with greater emphasis. Thus you must have noticed that it was accentuation which enabled you to recognize the form }{\i\f211 l}{\b\i\f212 o}{\i\f211 queris}{\f208  (you speak) from the form }{\i\f211 loqu}{\b\i\f212 e}{\i\f211 ris}{\f208  (you will speak), i.e. to distinguish the }{\i\f211 present}{\f208  from the }{\i\f211 future}{\f208  tense.\par \par REMARK: These two forms differ, moreover, by the quantity of their syllables (see the introduction):\par \par present tense: l}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 queris, (e is short);\par }{\f208\lang1036 future tense: loqu}{\b\f210\lang1036 \u233\'8e}{\f208\lang1036 ris, (\u233\'8e is long).\par \par }{\b\f210 If you have the audio recordings}{\f208 , listen several times to the lesson while repeating each sentence in your mind. Then recite it }{\i\f211 aloud}{\f208 , following the book, while imitating }{\i\f211 what you heard}{\f208  as well as you can. Finally, adjust your pronunciation, by again li}{\f208 stening to the audio recording.}{\par }}